> If there is a nethack port on say, an Android device,
> wouldn't the source for that port have to be made available?

It depends on whether the license has a "you must provide the
modified source" clause.  A lot of (perhaps even most) open-source
licenses don't have such a clause (I think the BSD license is the
most famous example, but the MIT and Apache licenses and various
others are similar), but some do (most famously the Gnu GPL).

Based on this:
http://nethackwiki.com/wiki/NetHack_General_Public_License

It looks like policy number 3 is indeed such a requirement.  IANAL,
but that's how it reads to me.  So yeah, if my interpretation is
correct, the source for any port would have to be either included
with every copy distributed or else made available generally.

It also appears to my (legally untrained) eye that 2b explicitly
forbids selling (licenses for) modified versions for money, even
with source included -- something the Gnu GPL does not forbid.  This
probably means that if someone wanted to sell a hardware device with
NetHack pre-installed they could potentially run into legal trouble,
if somebody with enough money to hire a lawyer wanted to make an
issue of it.  People who sell e.g. Debian DVDs that happen to
include NetHack are probably in the clear under 2c, however.  The
line between these two situations could potentially get blurry
enough to keep a dozen lawyers busy for a year or two (if there
were ever enough money or equivalent value involved to encourage
said lawyers to see that as a potentially worthwhile use of their
time; such a scenario is difficult to imagine but not impossible).

(Imagine, for example, that part of the purchase price of some
hardware device includes a subscription to an otherwise-inaccessible
online app repository that offers a couple of pieces of proprietary
software (say, a personal information manager, maps, and apps for
sending and recieving email and SMS messages) and a NetHack port.
Further imagine that the NetHack port includes the source, but the
device doesn't directly provide you any way to actually access
arbitrary things on its filesystem or transfer files off.  Imagine
that it's possible to jailbreak the device but doing so changes the
checksum on an important system file, which voids the warranty and
also causes the app store, and all apps retrieved from the app
store, to refuse to run...)


